; ==========================================================================
; HaniCore Bosch ME7.1.1 - the flash layout, and why it is this one.
;
; The image is 1 MiB at CPU 0x800000-0x8FFFFF; file offset X is CPU 0x800000+X.
; It is laid out around the 29F800's ERASE SECTORS, not around whatever the
; OEM happened to do:
;
;   file 0x000000-0x00FFFF   SA0..SA3     boot contract. The external boot
;                                         block plus the ROMSYS header. The
;                                         header is HaniCore-GENERATED; the
;                                         block below it is retained, because
;                                         it is executed code and deleting it
;                                         is a proven dead ECU.
;   file 0x010000-0x01FFFF   SA4          retained. The parameter-page window
;                                         whose two-word bootrom sum is
;                                         anchored here. Bosch already leaves
;                                         this sector OUT of the program-page
;                                         record - the hole between its two
;                                         summing loops - and V53 uses that
;                                         same idea for the store below.
;   file 0x020000-0x0201FF                HaniCore's INTERRUPT VECTOR TABLE.
;                                         Not linked here - Keil parks VECTAB
;                                         inside our own segment and the build
;                                         tool relocates it, exactly as the
;                                         ME7.5 target does.
;   [2026-10-06] SA6 is FCODE since V97 (see the FCODE class below); only
;   SA5 (0x020200-0x02FFFF) stays reserved for the loader.
;   file 0x020200-0x03FFFF   SA5..SA6     reserved for a resident recovery /
;                                         update loader.
;   file 0x040000-0x04FFFF   SA7          EVERYTHING HANICORE EXECUTES. One
;                                         whole 64 KiB erase sector, so a
;                                         firmware update erases exactly one
;                                         sector and can touch nothing else.
;                                         MOVED HERE FROM SA17 AT V53 - see
;                                         below.
;   file 0x050000-0x05FFFF   SA8          reserved for code growth, and it is
;                                         reserved rather than merely free:
;                                         it is inside the boot page record,
;                                         so code may spill into it without
;                                         moving the record boundary again.
;   file 0x060000-0x0FFFFF   SA9..SA18    THE APPEND-ONLY CALIBRATION STORE.
;                                         Eighty 8 KiB cells, ABOVE the
;                                         bootrom program-page record.
;
; WHY THE CODE MOVED, V53, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE RELAYOUT.
;
; The bootrom's program-page record samples the first and last word of every
; 8 KiB page it covers, and the boot block REFUSES to enter HaniCore if the
; stored sum stops matching - physically proven on 2026-09-05, one programmed
; word cost a bootmode reflash. An AMD embedded erase pre-programs a whole
; sector to 0x00 before erasing it, so every sampled word in that sector is
; wrong for the ~2 s the erase takes. And this board ties the 29F800's RESET
; pin (44-pin SO pin 44) to RSTIN_FR, so SWRST_SM does not merely interrupt
; the CPU - it ABORTS the erase and leaves the sector indeterminate.
;
; So while the store lived in SA7/SA8 it could not be reclaimed without
; risking the ECU, and the store was capped at sixteen Burns for that reason.
;
; The record's coverage is NOT hardcoded in the boot block: it is read out of
; the ROMSYS header this build generates (header+0x14 is the second range's
; limit, and it has exactly one consumer, boot block 0x72D2). Moving that
; limit down to 0x85FFFF takes SA9..SA18 out of the record entirely. The
; summing loop offers only TWO ranges, so the excluded region has to be
; contiguous at the top - which is why the code sector had to come down here.
; Every executable byte is still covered exactly as strongly as before, and
; tools/me711_bootsum_coverage_proof.py proves it by EXECUTING the real Bosch
; routine and comparing address sets.
;
; NOTE WHAT IS ABSENT, compared with the ME7.5 linker script: there is no list
; of windows dodging 8 KiB page boundaries. That script had to avoid the words
; the bootrom's program-page sum samples, because it KEPT the donor's stored
; sum and therefore had to keep the donor's sampled bytes. This build GENERATES
; that sum from its own content, so the sampled words are ours to use and the
; code region is one contiguous range.
; ==========================================================================

; Parked where the build tool expects it - see relocate_vectors() in
; tools/build_hanicore_me711.py. A table linked at 0x000000 would
; overwrite the boot block's own reset vector and kill the ECU; the CPU does
; not read it there anyway, because the first-level table forwards interrupt N
; to seg82:N*4.
VECTAB (0x840000)

; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PEC POINTER FILE, 0xFCE0-0xFCFF, IS CARVED OUT OF EVERY CLASS BELOW.
;
; SRCP0/DSTP0 .. SRCP7/DSTP7 live at 0x00FCE0-0x00FCFF. They are REGISTERS,
; not RAM, even though they sit inside the internal-RAM window the compiler
; allocates near data from. Authority: reg167.h, Infineon's own header,
; in the M744 stand-alone ECU project Latest version FW sourceCode/reg167.h
; lines 352-369.
;
; L166 works around 0xFC00-0xFC1F on its own, because the GPR bank is a real
; absolute segment (?C_MAINREGISTERS) it can see. NOTHING declares the PEC
; pointers, so until this carve-out existed the linker allocated ordinary
; variables straight on top of them.
;
; WHAT THAT COST. In V29 hc_asic_state landed at 0xFCE2 = DSTP0. Every call to
; asic_start_transfer() programs the PEC destination pointer, so every SSC
; block transfer overwrote the ASIC state machine's own state with the low
; byte of a buffer address - 0xF0B0 -> state 176, a value no case in the
; switch matches. The handshake could never reach its runtime state.
; me711_boot_test lost six checks to it and the symptom pointed at the ASIC
; rather than at the linker.
;
; IT IS OLDER THAN V29. V26, the only physically verified image, places
; nothing here. V27A already placed sixteen variables here - engine ones -
; and passed its gates anyway, because corrupting eng_ti_us is invisible to a
; test that never runs the engine. V29 only made it visible by moving a
; variable the state machine reads onto DSTP0.
;
; The carve-out costs 32 bytes of near RAM and cannot make a correct
; allocation incorrect: it only removes address space the silicon already
; owned. If a build ever fails to fit because of it, that build was relying
; on corrupt RAM.
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLASSES (
  ; THIRD MOVE, 2026-09-06, V42. ICODE holds ?C_STARTUP_CODE and nothing else:
  ; 208 bytes of a 3584-byte reservation, unchanged since V17. The V42 writer's
  ; retry, settle and diary cost 352 bytes of NCODE that the 6 free bytes left
  ; over from the V40 move could not hold, and taking them out of NCONST again
  ; would leave both classes inside 400 bytes of their reservations - the exact
  ; state that made this an L5 spill twice in three days. So the 3 KiB ICODE has
  ; never used is given to code instead. ?C_STARTUP stays at 0x840200, which is
  ; what the application entry pointer at 0x008000 names and what
  ; tools/me711_boot_contract.py checks. Everything is still inside SA7.
  ICODE  (0x840200-0x8403FF),
  ; NCODE grew past 0x84DFFF when the Burn was made bounded (2026-09-04), and
  ; the linker says so with L5 SECTION LOCATED OUTSIDE CLASS AREA rather than
  ; silently spilling - so the boundary moves DELIBERATELY, and every move is
  ; recorded here. NCONST held 4447 bytes of an 8 KiB reservation; giving 2 KiB
  ; of that back to code left 54094 of 55296 used for code and 4447 of 6144
  ; for const, and keeps the whole application inside SA7 - the invariant that
  ; matters: an application update erases exactly one sector and touches
  ; nothing else.
  ;
  ; SECOND MOVE, 2026-09-06, V40 RESETLESS BURN. The V39 layout left exactly
  ; 28 BYTES of NCODE headroom - ?PR?HC_CONFIG_GENERATED ended at 0x84E7E3 -
  ; so the 42 bytes of tick-gated yield that keep a LIVE flash commit inside
  ; the 10 ms raster spilled the section out of the class. NCONST's own last
  ; section ends at 0x84F92D, 1746 bytes short of its reservation; 1 KiB of
  ; that moves to code. NCODE 0x841000-0x84EBFF is 56320 bytes for 55310 used,
  ; NCONST 0x84EC00-0x84FFFF is 5120 for 4397. Both still inside SA7.
  ;
  ; NCODE 0x840400-0x84EDFF is 59904 bytes for 56666 used; NCONST
  ; 0x84EE00-0x84FFFF is 4608 for 4402.
  ;
  ; FOURTH MOVE, 2026-09-13, V53. THE APPLICATION NOW SPANS SA7 AND SA8.
  ;
  ; The reclaim - a relocated sector eraser, the slice state machine and the
  ; victim search - is about 1.8 KiB of code, and SA7 had roughly 700 bytes
  ; left. Trimming the victim search to read record HEADERS instead of
  ; CRC-checking every consumed cell made it far faster but very slightly
  ; BIGGER, which is worth knowing: the inline 32-bit header reads cost more
  ; than the call they replaced. It stays trimmed for the runtime, not the size.
  ;
  ; So the boundary moves into SA8 rather than the feature being cut, and SA8
  ; is exactly what was reserved for this at the start of V53 - it is inside
  ; the bootrom page record, so growing into it changes NOTHING about the
  ; record's boundary and therefore nothing about the store above it. That was
  ; the whole reason to reserve a protected sector rather than leave it free.
  ;
  ; WHAT IT DOES COST, said plainly: "an application update erases exactly ONE
  ; sector" becomes TWO. Both are inside the record, neither is the store, and
  ; the resident update loader reserved at 0x020200-0x03FFFF is untouched. The
  ; alternative was to keep re-tuning this boundary every time a feature lands,
  ; which is the state that produced three L5 spills in a week.
  ;
  ; NCODE 0x840400-0x85BFFF is 113664 bytes for 60752 used; NCONST
  ; 0x85C000-0x85FFFF is 16384 for 4550. Both inside SA7+SA8, both inside the
  ; boot page record, and there is room for the engine work still to land.
  ;
  ; FIFTH MOVE, 2026-09-15, V55. THAT LAST SENTENCE WAS NOT TRUE AND THE RANGE
  ; ABOVE WAS A FICTION.
  ;
  ; NCODE is a single CGROUP. L166 refuses one larger than a segment:
  ;
  ;     *** ERROR L116: SIZE OF CGROUP EXCEEDS 64K
  ;         GROUP:   NCODE (1)
  ;
  ; so the usable window has never been 113664 bytes - it is 0x840400 to
  ; 0x84FFFF, 64512 bytes, and writing 0x85BFFF here only hid that until
  ; something asked for more. Restoring hcsched_dry.c's crank-angle planner
  ; asked for 3.6 KiB against 2.9 KiB free and the error arrived with no
  ; warning history behind it.
  ;
  ; THE RANGE NOW SAYS WHAT THE LINKER WILL ACTUALLY DO, so the next spill is
  ; an honest L5 naming the real boundary instead of an L116 naming a group.
  ; SA8 is still reserved and still inside the boot page record; reaching it
  ; needs FCODE - a second code class and a memory model per module - not a
  ; larger number here. That is a deliberate piece of work with its own
  ; qualification, and it is written down in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md rather
  ; than discovered again.
  ;
  ; What paid for V55 instead: the 117 unclaimed-trap catch-alls in
  ; hcvec_me711.c stopped calling hc_rst_vector_at() in hcconfig.c and write
  ; the same two reset-diary words themselves. A Keil C166 `interrupt`
  ; function that calls out of its module saves the whole register bank, and
  ; that convention cost 4870 bytes of this sector - the module's own header
  ; had warned about it since the ME7.5 target hit it first.
  NCODE  (0x840400-0x84FFFF),
  ; V97: FCODE CONTINUES IN SA6. SA8 was full (the knock driver spilled
  ; 1168 bytes into 0x860000 - the persistence store's first sector - as an
  ; L5). SA6 is blank and inside the boot page record; 0x838000-0x83800F is
  ; left out because the builder writes the boot block's defensive far
  ; pointer at 0x838004 (bootmode 0xDD). The resident-loader reservation
  ; (docs/PLATFORM.md) keeps SA5. A firmware update now erases
  ; SA6..SA8.
  ; FCODE ends inside SA8, below the constant classes; each class keeps its
  ; >=1024-byte reserve (me711_resource_budget_proof). The store sectors
  ; (SA9.., 0x860000) are never used for code.
  FCODE  (0x830000-0x837FFF, 0x838010-0x83FFFF, 0x850000-0x85B4BF),
  ; V98r2: HCONST - the generated preset item tables (hc_presets.c,
  ; HC_PRESET_MEM huge) had filled NCONST; 16 KB out of FCODE's SA8 tail
  ; (FCODE used 3 KB of 0x850000-0x85BFFF).
  HCONST (0x85B4C0-0x85E1FF),
  ; NCONST stays inside the near-constant page 0x85C000-0x85FFFF.
  NCONST (0x85E200-0x85FFFF),

  ; Near data. 0x00F200-0x00FDFF is the RAM the boot block TESTS at every
  ; reset (0x77B4-0x7810 walks 0xFDFA down to 0xF200 and resets on a
  ; mismatch); the OEM application keeps variables in 0xF200-0xF5FF too.
  ;
  ; STACK LAYOUT (hcsafety.h):
  ;   0xF200-0xF27F  near data, overflow range (NDATA0 below) - the upper
  ;                  IRAM keeps room for the interrupt register banks
  ;   0xF280-0xF2BF  runtime health record (hcrtmon711.h, fixed address)
  ;   0xF2C0-0xF33F  K-line transport state (hcserial711.c, fixed address)
  ;   0xF340-0xF37F  user-stack GUARD BAND - nothing may be placed here
  ;   0xF380-0xF57F  ?C_USERSTACK, 512 bytes, the only NDATA section
  ;   0xF580-0xF7FF  system stack, 634 usable (STKOV 0xF586, STKUN 0xF800)
  ;   0xF800-        C near data, as before
  ;   (the K-line PEC6 ring is XRAM 0xE0A0-0xE0FF, the OEM's own place)
  NDATA  (0xF380-0xF57F),
  NDATA0 (0xF200-0xF27F, 0xF800-0xFCDF, 0xFD00-0xFDFF),
  SDATA  (0xF800-0xFCDF, 0xFD00-0xFDFF),
  SDATA0 (0xF800-0xFCDF, 0xFD00-0xFDFF),
  IDATA  (0xF800-0xFCDF, 0xFD00-0xFDFF),
  IDATA0 (0xF800-0xFCDF, 0xFD00-0xFDFF),
  ; V77: the cold modules compiled RENAMECLASS(NDATA0=XNDATA0) - see below.
  XNDATA0 (0xE000-0xE09F, 0xE200-0xE7FF)
)
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; V97. THE CLASS GAINED 0xE000-0xE09F (160 bytes): the boost, idle, DTC
; and limp modules overflowed 0xE200-0xE7FF by 142 bytes (L5). The range
; ends below 0xE0A0 because the OEM placed its K-line PEC receive ring at
; 0xE0A2 (hcpecrx_me711.c, not linked yet - PEC6 is unwired) and below the
; ASIC PEC frame at 0xE100. Same XRAM, same page 3, same DPP2: nothing
; about the instructions changes. near_ram_hazard_proof checks both ranges.
;
; V77. COLD NEAR DATA LIVES IN THE ON-CHIP XRAM, 0xE200-0xE7FF.
;
; Near data had 112 bytes free against a 192-byte engineering reserve. The
; 2 KB XRAM at 0xE000-0xE7FF is:
;   * ENABLED BEFORE ANY C RUNS - start_me711.a66 writes SYSCON 0xE644/0xE244
;     (XPEN set) right after BUSCON0;
;   * CLEARED EXACTLY LIKE IRAM - the boot block's RAM test (0x7788-0x7814)
;     makes the same three passes, ~addr / addr / ZERO, over 0xFDFA..0xF200
;     and 0xE7FE..0xE000, so a variable here starts at the same zero on the
;     same reset paths as one in IRAM;
;   * IN THE SAME 16 KB PAGE AS 0xF700 - page 3, DPP2's NDATA group page - so
;     every access compiles to the SAME instruction with the SAME DPP. L166
;     refuses the link if the group ever leaves that page;
;   * used by nothing else except the ASIC PEC frame at 0xE100-0xE1EB
;     (TGT_ASIC_FRAME), which this range starts above.
;
; THE MECHANISM is build_me711.bat compiling exactly those modules with
; RENAMECLASS(NDATA0=XNDATA0): their near-data section keeps its NDATA group
; (so DPP2 and every instruction are unchanged) and only its CLASS, and so
; its range, differs.
;
; ONLY COLD MODULES MOVE, chosen so the tooth path does not change at all:
; the TunerStudio protocol state, the main loop's bookkeeping, the bench, the
; ASIC start/run counters and SSC state, UMKOM, the pump and relay
; lifecycles, the config manager and the validator. The scheduler, the
; decoder, the HAL output route, hc_cfg[], the K-line receive ring, the
; engine outputs and the safety permissions STAY in IRAM.
; near_ram_hazard_proof checks this range never meets the ASIC frame.
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTIONS (
  ; The user stack is PINNED. Without a pin L166 moved it to 0xFD00 the
  ; moment the class had room, and an overflow from there would run DOWN into
  ; the PEC pointer file at 0xFCE0. It sits BELOW the system
  ; stack (0xF380-0xF57F) so an overflow runs into the guard band at
  ; 0xF340-0xF37F and never into interrupt return frames.
  ?C_USERSTACK%NDATA (0xF380)
)
